WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.702 --> 00:00:18.780
The police station was nearby, so the crowd could only accompany Oliver through two or three streets.

2
00:00:18.781 --> 00:00:23.776
But even that short distance seemed to please a tall man wrapped in a dark cloak.

3
00:00:23.777 --> 00:00:31.943
As Oliver was led into the court, the man's lips curled in a cruel smile. He disappeared when the crowd broke up.

4
00:00:31.944 --> 00:00:39.769
Oliver was taken to a cell. The old gentleman whose handkerchief had been stolen was instructed to wait in a small room.

5
00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:49.166
"There's something about that boy's face that touches me," the old gentleman said to himself. "Could he be innocent? Where have I seen that face before?"

6
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:58.079
The old gentleman thought back over the many people he'd known. "No," he said at last. "It must be my imagination."

7
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:04.587
He picked up the book from the bookshop and started reading again. Soon he was led into the courtroom.

8
00:01:04.588 --> 00:01:11.301
Mr. Fang, the magistrate, sat at one end of the room while Oliver sat trembling in a wooden pen.

9
00:01:11.302 --> 00:01:15.068
"Who are you?" Mr. Fang demanded gruffly.

10
00:01:15.069 --> 00:01:18.235
"My name is Brownlow," said the old gentleman.

11
00:01:18.236 --> 00:01:21.727
"And what crime are you charged with?"

12
00:01:21.728 --> 00:01:26.576
"I'm not charged with any crime! My handkerchief was stolen."

13
00:01:26.577 --> 00:01:31.116
"Swear this man in," Mr. Fang instructed a court officer.

14
00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:38.870
And so Mr. Brownlow began to tell his story. He explained that he had chased Oliver because he saw the boy running away.

15
00:01:38.871 --> 00:01:44.631
He hoped the magistrate would be lenient if this boy wasn't the thief but merely connected with thieves.

16
00:01:44.632 --> 00:01:54.623
In conclusion Mr. Brownlow said, "The boy has been hurt already." Then looking at Oliver, he said forcefully, "And I fear . . . I really fear that he's ill."

17
00:01:54.624 --> 00:02:00.266
"Oh, yes, I'm sure he's ill," said Mr. Fang with a sneer.

18
00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:08.607
He looked at Oliver and said sternly, "I'll have none of your tricks here, you young vagabond. What's your name?"

19
00:02:08.608 --> 00:02:16.117
Oliver tried to reply, but his tongue failed him. The whole room seemed to spin, and he was deadly pale.

20
00:02:16.118 --> 00:02:23.355
"What's your name, you hardened scoundrel?" demanded Mr. Fang. "Officer, what's his name?"

21
00:02:23.356 --> 00:02:27.593
This comment was addressed to a police officer standing next to Oliver.

22
00:02:27.594 --> 00:02:34.418
The policeman bent over the boy and repeated the question. But he realized that Oliver couldn't reply.

23
00:02:34.419 --> 00:02:40.506
He also knew that this would make the magistrate angry and likely add to the severity of Oliver's punishment.

24
00:02:40.507 --> 00:02:45.473
"He says his name's Tom White, your worship," replied the kindhearted police officer.

25
00:02:45.474 --> 00:02:49.569
"Where does he live?" asked Mr. Fang.

26
00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:55.061
"Wherever he can, your worship," replied the officer, again pretending to hear Oliver's answer.

27
00:02:55.062 --> 00:02:57.743
"Does he have any parents?"

28
00:02:57.744 --> 00:03:02.727
"He says they died in his infancy, your worship," said the officer, making a guess.

29
00:03:02.728 --> 00:03:11.890
At this point Oliver raised his head and looked around with imploring eyes. "Please, may I have a drink of water?" he asked feebly.

30
00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:18.556
"Stuff and nonsense!" said Mr. Fang. "Don't try to make a fool of me."

31
00:03:18.557 --> 00:03:22.199
"I think he really is ill, Your Worship," said the officer.

32
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.390
"I know better," said Mr. Fang.

33
00:03:25.391 --> 00:03:32.620
Mr. Brownlow had been watching Oliver with concern. "Take care of him, Officer. He'll fall down."

34
00:03:32.621 --> 00:03:38.863
"Step back, Officer," said Mr. Fang. "Let him fall if he likes."

35
00:03:38.864 --> 00:03:43.212
And with that, Oliver fainted and fell to the floor.

36
00:03:43.213 --> 00:03:48.696
The men in the room looked at each other, but no one dared to move without Mr. Fang's permission.

37
00:03:48.697 --> 00:03:58.680
"I knew he was pretending," Mr. Fang said in a self-satisfied way. "Let him lie there. He'll soon grow tired of his game and get up."

38
00:03:58.681 --> 00:04:03.172
"How do you propose to deal with the case, sir?" asked the clerk in a low voice.

39
00:04:03.173 --> 00:04:13.082
"He is committed to prison for three months," said Mr. Fang. "And sentenced to hard labor, of course. Everyone, clear the courtroom."

40
00:04:13.083 --> 00:04:18.328
The door was opened and a couple of men prepared to carry the unconscious boy to his cell.

41
00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:23.090
Suddenly an old man rushed into the courtroom and advanced toward the magistrate.

42
00:04:23.091 --> 00:04:30.495
"Stop, stop! Don't take him away!" The new arrival was breathless with hurry.

43
00:04:30.496 --> 00:04:40.301
"What is this? Who is this? Turn this man out. Clear the courtroom!" cried Mr. Fang.

44
00:04:40.302 --> 00:04:55.134
"I will speak," declared the man. "I will not be turned away. I saw everything. I own the bookshop. I demand that you hear my evidence, Mr. Fang. You must not refuse me."

45
00:04:55.135 --> 00:04:58.626
The man stood in front of the bench, breathing heavily.

46
00:04:58.627 --> 00:05:05.604
"Swear him in," growled Mr. Fang. "Now, man, what have you got to say?"

47
00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:11.112
"This gentleman was reading outside my shop." The man indicated Mr. Brownlow.

48
00:05:11.113 --> 00:05:25.204
"I saw three boys—two others and the prisoner here—loitering across the way. The robbery was committed by another boy. I saw it, and I also saw that this boy was perfectly amazed and stupefied by it."

49
00:05:25.205 --> 00:05:31.458
The bookshop owner took a deep breath and then proceeded to relate the exact circumstances of the robbery.

50
00:05:31.459 --> 00:05:36.870
Mr. Fang was clearly annoyed. "Why didn't you come here before?"

51
00:05:36.871 --> 00:05:41.434
"I didn't have anyone to mind the shop," replied the man.

52
00:05:41.435 --> 00:05:49.437
"Everybody who could have helped me had joined in the chase. I couldn't get anybody until five minutes ago, and I've run all the way here."

53
00:05:49.438 --> 00:05:53.898
"This man was reading, was he?" Mr. Fang pointed to Mr. Brownlow.

54
00:05:53.899 --> 00:05:57.001
"Yes, the very book he has in his hand."

55
00:05:57.002 --> 00:06:00.826
"Oh, that book. Is it paid for?"

56
00:06:00.827 --> 00:06:04.747
"No, it is not," said the bookseller with a smile.

57
00:06:04.748 --> 00:06:09.572
"Dear me, I forgot all about that!" exclaimed Mr. Brownlow.

58
00:06:09.573 --> 00:06:20.320
"Well, you're a nice person to bring charges against a poor boy!" said Mr. Fang. "You obtained that book under very suspicious and disreputable circumstances.

59
00:06:20.321 --> 00:06:27.399
You're lucky that the owner of that book has decided not to prosecute you. Let this be a lesson to you."

60
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:31.375
"What! But I meant to pay!" exclaimed Mr. Brownlow.

61
00:06:31.376 --> 00:06:37.891
"The boy is discharged. Clear the courtroom!" said Mr. Fang.

62
00:06:37.892 --> 00:06:42.113
"Officers, do you hear? Clear the courtroom."

63
00:06:42.114 --> 00:06:49.394
An indignant Mr. Brownlow was escorted from the magistrate's court with the book in one hand and his bamboo cane in the other.

64
00:06:49.395 --> 00:06:52.965
But when he reached the pavement, his rage evaporated.

65
00:06:52.966 --> 00:06:58.544
Little Oliver Twist lay on the ground with his shirt unbuttoned and his face a deadly white.

66
00:06:58.545 --> 00:07:05.274
"Poor boy, poor boy!" said Mr. Brownlow, bending over him. "Call a coach, somebody."

67
00:07:05.275 --> 00:07:12.184
A coach arrived and Oliver was carefully laid on one seat. Mr. Brownlow got in and sat on the other one.

68
00:07:12.185 --> 00:07:15.859
"May I accompany you?" said the bookseller, looking in.

69
00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:26.525
"Bless me, yes, my dear sir," said Mr. Brownlow. "I still have this unhappy book. Jump in. Poor fellow! There's no time to lose."

70
00:07:26.526 --> 00:07:32.915
The bookseller got into the coach, and away they drove toward Mr. Brownlow's home.
